OBSERVATION: Rounds 3, 4, and 5 read as three unrelated findings but are one repeated shape, verified by reading each guard directly. In every case the machinery that WOULD answer correctly already exists elsewhere in the same file, and a separate, narrower closed-set gate sitting in front of it fails to recognize a valid input variant and blocks the route: (1) round 3 — looksCodeish() (src/chat.mjs:854, regex /[a-z][A-Z]|[_./]|()/ ) flags any CamelCase compound as code-ish and excludes it from the bare-meta-fact fallback lanes (2b/2c, ~7212-7290) that DO know how to answer "what is X"; (2) round 4 — CONTEXT_WORDS (src/chat.mjs:87, a literal Set of 4 singular pronouns "it"/"this"/"that"/"here") has no plural/ordinal members, and even within the set, ask.mjs resolveObject (~2925) never consults contextId/focus for object-position "that" the way subject-position "it" resolution (chat.mjs ~7008) does; (3) round 5 — COUNT_NOUNS (src/chat.mjs:431, a literal class-noun map) is checked by answerCount guard (~547) but ask-vocab.mjs EDGE_NOUN_TO_METRIC (~855), which already maps "tests"/"importers"/"callers" to edge kinds, is never consulted from that guard. All three are enumerable closed sets (a regex charclass, a 4-word Set, a noun-to-class map) that lag behind sibling machinery already capable of the correct answer once the gate lets the query through, and all three were correctly deferred (per HANDOVER.md) rather than patched, because the gates are shared, high-blast-radius chokepoints (isConversational/looksCodeish, ask.mjs core relation grammar) with documented past regressions from touching them casually.
RECOMMENDED ACTION: This predicts, not just describes — the remaining 4 fast-loop rounds (drill-down and beyond) are likely to keep finding the same shape (a valid phrasing variant excluded by some other closed-set gate/table this codebase has many of), not new failure classes. Two low-cost moves worth taking before HANDOVER.md accumulates a 6th/7th near-duplicate bullet: (a) when writing the PLAN_*.md docs promised for findings 3-5, consider one shared "closed-set gate coverage" design doc (or a shared section) instead of three fully separate ones — the fix pattern (audit a specific gate, decide what new members/table-lookups are safe to add, add regression tests for the existing exclusions) is the same exercise each time, so a single design pass may cover most of the remaining scope at once; (b) if round 6+ produces a 4th instance of this exact shape, treat that as confirmation rather than a new independent finding — log it tersely referencing this entry rather than re-deriving the pattern from scratch.
